WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.900
How can a string of letters reveal what an unknown protein does?

2
00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:10.120
Children with smooth brains led researchers to a human gene called LIS1.

3
00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:14.300
Its sequence alone did not say what the protein does.

4
00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:22.770
Sequence alignment slides two letter strips past each other until matching positions

5
00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:26.770
line up. The clue came from a mould gene, nudF.

6
00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:34.830
In 1995, its protein matched human LIS1 at 42 percent of aligned positions.

7
00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:38.970
Chance rarely matches that much along a whole protein.

8
00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:44.170
So it pointed to homology: inheritance from one shared ancestor.

9
00:00:45.333 --> 00:00:49.513
A match lets knowledge from one organism become a clue in another.

10
00:00:51.013 --> 00:00:55.773
Nud stands for nuclear distribution: mould mutants leave their nuclei bunched.

11
00:00:56.973 --> 00:01:03.013
Other nud genes encode parts of cytoplasmic dynein, a motor that moves nuclei along

12
00:01:03.013 --> 00:01:05.373
protein tracks called microtubules.

13
00:01:05.373 --> 00:01:10.713
Mammalian cell experiments confirmed that LIS1 binds dynein.

14
00:01:11.833 --> 00:01:15.493
Suppose two species share the exact same sequence feature.

15
00:01:16.773 --> 00:01:21.633
Does that guarantee they are closest relatives, or could another history explain it?

16
00:01:27.117 --> 00:01:30.357
A match alone cannot guarantee closest relatives.

17
00:01:31.637 --> 00:01:36.017
Two lineages can gain the same feature separately, a trap called convergence.

18
00:01:37.417 --> 00:01:41.117
Or both can keep an ancestral state, present before they split.

19
00:01:42.457 --> 00:01:47.397
Only a shared derived change, gained once on one shared branch, shows which

20
00:01:47.397 --> 00:01:48.417
relatives are closest.

21
00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:54.120
An outside relative, called an outgroup, suggests which state is ancestral.

22
00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:59.440
Its own lineage can change too, so studies compare several outgroups.

23
00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:03.920
One gene's tree can also differ from the species tree.

24
00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:08.500
Tools like Scansite predict where a phosphate group might attach.

25
00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.600
A predicted site stays a guess until experiments find the phosphate.

26
00:02:14.733 --> 00:02:20.653
In 1986, Robert Lefkowitz's laboratory at Duke and a Merck group cloned a

27
00:02:20.653 --> 00:02:23.993
beta-adrenergic receptor, which senses adrenaline.

28
00:02:25.393 --> 00:02:30.293
Its sequence resembled rhodopsin, the eye's light-catching pigment, seven

29
00:02:30.293 --> 00:02:32.573
water-avoiding membrane spans included.

30
00:02:33.953 --> 00:02:38.693
Every seven-span protein then known used light, so some guessed the plan meant light

31
00:02:38.693 --> 00:02:45.093
sensing. Instead, it marked receptors signaling through G proteins, relay proteins

32
00:02:45.093 --> 00:02:46.093
inside cells.

33
00:02:47.067 --> 00:02:51.467
In 1989, Michael Reth published a letter with no experiment.

34
00:02:52.827 --> 00:02:57.387
He lined up published tail sequences of partner chains from immune receptors.

35
00:02:58.867 --> 00:03:04.087
Chains from T cells, the B-cell receptor and the mast-cell receptor all shared one

36
00:03:04.087 --> 00:03:05.087
pattern.

37
00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:11.950
In 2007, Amit, Wides and Yarden compared the human genome with four invertebrate

38
00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:17.550
animals. Genomes differ in total gene number, so they adjusted for it.

39
00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:23.610
Per gene, humans had about 2.5 times as many EGF receptors.

40
00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:28.970
Total protein kinases were only about 1.2 times higher.

41
00:03:30.033 --> 00:03:35.793
From one unknown protein to whole genomes, shared sequences point to relatives and

42
00:03:35.793 --> 00:03:39.253
candidate functions, and experiments then confirm them.
